From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mpr, net, explorer: Map Network Drive asks for credentials; drives reconnect at sign-in

Mapping a share that refuses the signed-in user (a guest, or a domain user
the server does not know) showed "The folder cannot be reached. Access
denied." and stopped; the only way in was to tick "Connect using different
credentials" first. As Windows' Map Network Drive does, a refused share
now brings up "Enter network credentials" (for that server), asks again
with "Logon unsuccessful" while the name or password is wrong, and maps
the drive with the right ones; Cancel goes back to the dialog.

"Reconnect at sign-in" (ticked by default) remembered the drive under
HKCU\Network, but nothing ever reconnected it: a mapped drive was gone
after the next sign-in. Now:

- WNetRestoreConnection (a stub) connects remembered drives again: as the
  signed-in user (a guest, or the domain ticket), else with the name and
  password saved for the server;
- the prompt offers "Remember my credentials", saved for the server
  (Credential Manager, a domain password credential) once it took them;
- the shell reconnects the remembered drives at each sign-in, in a process
  of its own (rundll32 mpr.dll,SgRestoreConnections) so that a server out
  of reach does not hold up the desktop;
- NET USE takes /PERSISTENT:YES (remember the drive) and /SAVECRED (keep
  the name and password); NET USE X: /DELETE forgets a remembered drive,
  as on Windows; the remembered drive keeps the user name it was made with.

Test: test/mapcred-gate.sh with test/mapcred-probe.c and
test/mapcred-netmountd.py, a stand-in for sg-netmountd (mutants
SG_MUTANT_MAP_NO_CRED_RETRY and SG_MUTANT_NO_SAVED_CREDS in mapdlg.c,
SG_MUTANT_NO_RESTORE in wnet.c, SG_MUTANT_NO_SIGNIN_RESTORE in
programs/explorer/startup.c, SG_MUTANT_NO_SAVED_CREDS in programs/net/net.c).
The lab machine: \\fs1\private mapped as Y: after a wrong and a right
password, and back after sign-out and sign-in.
